reformulate:
You independently write a resolution rule for a forecasting question, then apply it to test scenarios. Do NOT output any probability or opinion about the outcome. Independent attempt number {{index}}.

Question: {{question}}
Resolution deadline: {{deadline}}
Test scenarios (numbered):
{{scenarios}}

Reply with only one JSON object: {"resolution_rule": "explicit YES/NO conditions, in the language of the question", "verdicts": [one of "YES", "NO", "AMBIGUOUS" for each scenario, in order]}

apply_rule:
Apply this resolution rule literally to each test scenario. Do NOT output any probability or opinion about the outcome.

Question: {{question}}
Resolution rule: {{rule}}
Test scenarios (numbered):
{{scenarios}}

Reply with only one JSON object: {"verdicts": [one of "YES", "NO", "AMBIGUOUS" for each scenario, in order]}

extract:
You extract facts from one source document for a forecasting question. Extraction run {{index}}. Rules: extract ONLY facts explicitly stated in the document; never infer, never estimate likelihoods, never output probabilities or confidence levels.

Question: {{question}}
Resolution rule: {{rule}}
Forecast date (UTC): {{now}}
Document title: {{title}}
Document URL: {{url}}
Document publication date: {{published_at}}
Document text:
<<<
{{text}}
>>>

Reply with only one JSON object: {"triplets": [{"fact": "one factual statement, in the language of the document", "date": "ISO-8601 date of the fact (not of the article)", "primary_source": "the original producer of the information (institution, pollster, agency, company, author of the study), NOT the outlet relaying it", "direction": "yes" if the fact makes a YES resolution more plausible, "no" if it makes NO more plausible, "neutral" otherwise, "evidence_type": one of "statistic", "poll", "official_statement", "expert_opinion", "market_price", "scheduled_event", "osint", "scientific", "news_report", "other", "mentions_forecasting_tool": true if the fact comes from a published forecast by an automated forecasting tool}]}. Return at most 8 triplets, most relevant first.
